

The file named menu is the menu in the plain text file format. The files
named menu.?? (?? is usually a two-letter language code such as pt, but
longer names such as zh_CN are also used) are translations of the same
menu. Localized menus are picked automatically from the locale: for a
locale like fr_FR, Window Maker tries menu.fr_FR and then menu.fr (the
.encoding suffix is stripped before the lookup). The locale can also be
set with the -locale command line option.

The plain text format is not run through an external cpp. It is parsed
by a built-in parser that supports a cpp-like subset of directives:
#include, #define (including function-like macros), #ifdef, #ifndef,
#else and #endif. The predefined macros live in the wmmacros file
(generated from wmmacros.in).

The plmenu file is the same menu, but in the property list format.
This menu can be edited with WPrefs.app, which supports input methods
(fcitx5/ibus through xcb-imdkit, or a compose table for dead keys), so
it can also be used for menus in languages such as Japanese.

The default WMRootMenu installed by "make install" is generated from
plmenu. To use the menu in the property list format (plmenu) as your
root menu, just copy plmenu over $WMAKER_USER_ROOT/Defaults/WMRootMenu.
To use the plain text menu, place the path for the menu in
$WMAKER_USER_ROOT/Defaults/WMRootMenu, enclosed in double quotes (").
Ex: "$WMAKER_USER_DATA/menu"

Instructions for editing the plain text menus can be found in the menu
file itself, kindly translated to your tongue for your convenience, by
the translation contributors.

There is a script to convert plain text menus to property list menus
in the util directory, named wm-oldmenu2new. It is installed on PATH
by "make install".

If you are unsure which format to use, stick with the plain text menus
as they are more flexible. If for some psychological reason you are
scared by text based configuration, use the property list menu.


